import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Why the arrow calculus kernel is closed","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/explanation/arrow-calculus-kernel.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/explanation/arrow-calculus-kernel.md"}`),a={name:`handl/explanation/arrow-calculus-kernel.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="why-the-arrow-calculus-kernel-is-closed" tabindex="-1">Why the arrow calculus kernel is closed <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-arrow-calculus-kernel-is-closed" aria-label="Permalink to “Why the arrow calculus kernel is closed”">​</a></h1><p>The arrow calculus kernel has exactly six combinators:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Combinator</th><th>Constructs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>arr</code></td><td>Lift a pure function into an arrow</td></tr><tr><td><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> (<code>seq</code>)</td><td>Sequential composition</td></tr><tr><td><code>first</code></td><td>Fan-out: run on the first of a pair</td></tr><tr><td><code>choice</code></td><td>Route on <code>Either</code>: <code>Left</code> to <code>l</code>, <code>Right</code> to <code>r</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>loop</code></td><td>Feedback (not iteration)</td></tr><tr><td><code>app</code></td><td>Higher-order arrow application</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are the only nodes the kernel knows about. The set is <strong>closed</strong>: new language features do not add new combinators. This is a deliberate design choice, not an oversight.</p><h2 id="why-closed" tabindex="-1">Why closed <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-closed" aria-label="Permalink to “Why closed”">​</a></h2><p>An open kernel — one where each new feature introduces a new combinator — accumulates special cases. Every combinator must be understood by every consumer of the kernel: the type checker, the refinement inference engine, every interpreter (tree-walk, bytecode, cranelift), the pretty-printer, the serialiser, and every handler that interprets a program. Each new combinator multiplies the surface area that those consumers must cover, and each one is a new place for the consumers to disagree.</p><p>A closed kernel inverts that pressure. New features are expressed as <strong>combinations</strong> of the existing six, or as <strong>desugaring</strong> to them, or as <strong>normalisation</strong> that eliminates the feature before the kernel ever sees it. The consumers stay simple because the kernel stays small. The cost of a new feature is paid once, at the boundary where it is translated into the kernel — not by every consumer downstream.</p><h2 id="the-cost-of-an-open-kernel" tabindex="-1">The cost of an open kernel <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cost-of-an-open-kernel" aria-label="Permalink to “The cost of an open kernel”">​</a></h2><p>Consider what would have happened if the module system (ADR-0069) had introduced two new kernel combinators — say <code>ModuleRef</code> and <code>Project</code> — to represent cross-file references and field projections:</p><ul><li>The <code>Scheme</code> enum gains two variants.</li><li>Every <code>match</code> over <code>Scheme</code> in the codebase must handle them, or fail to compile.</li><li>The refinement inference engine must learn what contracts <code>ModuleRef</code> and <code>Project</code> carry.</li><li>Every interpreter must decide what <code>ModuleRef</code> <em>means</em> at runtime — load a file? look up a registry? fail?</li><li>Serialisation must round-trip them.</li><li>The pretty-printer must render them.</li></ul><p>None of those consumers should care about modules. Modules are a <em>source-level</em> concern; by the time a program reaches the kernel, the question &quot;which file did this arrow come from?&quot; should already have been answered.</p><h2 id="how-features-enter-without-new-combinators" tabindex="-1">How features enter without new combinators <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-features-enter-without-new-combinators" aria-label="Permalink to “How features enter without new combinators”">​</a></h2><p>The module system is the worked example. A file is an arrow; an import is a projection; an export is a public name. None of that is in the kernel. Instead:</p><ul><li><strong>Parsing</strong> recognises <code>import</code> / <code>export</code> syntax and produces ordinary <code>Scheme</code> nodes, with module references and projections encoded as <code>arr</code> ops carrying string literals (<code>op: &quot;module_ref&quot;</code>, <code>op: &quot;project&quot;</code>).</li><li><strong>Normalisation Strategy A</strong> (<code>normalize_compile_time</code>) replaces those <code>module_ref</code> nodes with inlined records of the imported module&#39;s public arrows, and leaves <code>project</code> nodes as field accesses on the record.</li><li><strong>Normalisation Strategy B</strong> (<code>flatten_program</code>) inlines everything into one flat <code>Scheme</code> graph — no <code>module_ref</code>, no <code>project</code>, no external <code>app</code> references remain.</li></ul><p>By the time the program reaches inference or interpretation, it is plain arrows. The kernel never learns that modules existed.</p><p>This pattern generalises. A new source-level feature enters the system at one of three boundaries:</p><ol><li><strong>Desugaring</strong> — the parser rewrites the feature into existing combinators. Cheapest; the kernel is unaware.</li><li><strong>Normalisation</strong> — a pass over the <code>Scheme</code> graph eliminates the feature&#39;s encoding before downstream consumers run. Slightly more expensive; the encoding is visible between parsing and normalisation but not after.</li><li><strong>Handler interpretation</strong> — the feature is expressed as an <code>arr</code> op whose meaning is supplied by a handler, not by the kernel. The kernel carries the op opaquely; a host that does not understand it refuses to interpret it.</li></ol><p>None of these add a combinator. The kernel stays at six.</p><h2 id="when-the-set-should-grow" tabindex="-1">When the set should grow <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-the-set-should-grow" aria-label="Permalink to “When the set should grow”">​</a></h2><p>The set is closed, not frozen. A seventh combinator is justified only when a feature <strong>cannot</strong> be expressed as a combination of the existing six without losing a property the kernel must preserve — typically a property that every consumer relies on, such as &quot;the graph is finite&quot; or &quot;feedback is well-founded&quot;. Such cases are rare and should be governed by an ADR. The default answer is no: find a combination, a desugaring, or a normalisation. The kernel earns a new combinator only when the alternatives have been exhausted and the cost of the open set is demonstrably outweighed.</p><h2 id="relation-to-the-language-layer" tabindex="-1">Relation to the language layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#relation-to-the-language-layer" aria-label="Permalink to “Relation to the language layer”">​</a></h2><p>The same principle holds one layer up: because a language is data (see <a href="./language-as-data.html">Language as data</a>), expressive power comes from composing fragments, not from adding engine constructs. The arrow calculus kernel is the layer below — the calculus languages compile <em>to</em>. Both layers are closed by design, and for the same reason: a small, fixed core keeps every consumer honest.</p><h2 id="relation-to-adr-0106" tabindex="-1">Relation to ADR-0106 <a class="header-anchor" href="#relation-to-adr-0106" aria-label="Permalink to “Relation to ADR-0106”">​</a></h2><p><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/host/blob/project/adrs/0106-object-arrow-kernel.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ADR-0106: the object/arrow kernel</a> grounds this calculus. It keeps the closed six-combinator set as the computational substrate and folds it into a single object world: arrows are objects, <code>sym()</code>/<code>sym(A)</code> provide nominal identity, and <code>f&lt;...&gt;</code> is the one dimension specialization form. The six combinators are unchanged — they are the arrow fragment of ADR-0106&#39;s conceptual kernel.</p>`,24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};